cartridge "SPHEROIDS/Kevin Dixon/1990"

' SPHEROIDS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Spheroids" by Kevin Dixon, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette June 1990, p. 34 (Commodore 64: a BASIC arena builder, SPHERE.BAS,
' and a machine-language battle, SPHERE.ML, SYS 2624). Made for Amy Studio
' by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/sphere.dis, and its BASIC, orig/sphere.bas.bas, and
' watched in jsc64), the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision: shaded
' mounds and depressions, a swirling teleporter, smooth sprites, sound
' effects and a title tune.
'
' The game (as the C64 code plays it): two hovercraft (blue and green)
' duel in a walled arena with six spheroids. All movement runs in ticks
' (one pass of the C64's main loop, measured at about 43 a second). Every
' object has a speed of -9..9 on each axis; a phase counter 8..0 decides,
' through the C64's spread table 1,6,4,2,8,7,3,5, which ticks take a step
' (so a speed of n moves on min(n, 8) ticks in 9). Spheroids may step every
' tick, craft every second tick. The stick is read every 8 ticks: each
' held direction adds 1 to the craft's speed that way (up to 9); friction
' takes 1 off each axis every 12 ticks (craft) or 7 (spheroids). Walls
' turn a speed round AND add 2 to it (bounces speed things up). Every 5
' ticks (spheroids) or 8 (craft) the face under an object nudges its speed
' by 1 on both axes: a mound pushes away from its top; a depression's
' upper-left and lower-right faces push outward and its other two inward
' (the C64's colour codes; a saddle more than a pit); the teleporter throws the object to a random place.
' A craft that touches a plain (red) spheroid while holding none picks it
' up and carries it. Button + a direction (read on the stick's 8-tick
' beat) throws it at speed 9 that way (8 directions); with no direction
' nothing happens. A thrown spheroid takes the thrower's colour and is
' ARMED for 360 ticks (no friction, bouncing faster and faster); it cannot
' hurt its own thrower. An armed spheroid that touches the other craft
' blows it up: every spheroid turns plain at once, the burst lasts 112
' ticks, and after the pause the round restarts (craft back to their
' corners, spheroids scattered at random, the arena unchanged). Each
' player has 9 craft; the one who loses all 9 loses the battle. Arenas:
' OPEN, LATTICE, DIAMONDS, CYCLONE, QUADRATE, RANDOM (the BASIC builder's
' six layouts; each mound is a mound or a depression at random).
'
' Additions: a ONE PLAYER mode against the computer (the C64 game is for
' two players only), at two levels: EASY (it looks at the field every 16
' frames, aims loosely, dodges late) and NORMAL (every 8 frames). The
' computer drives the green craft through the same stick rules as a
' player.
'
' Liberties: the arena is 26 x 19 cells (the C64's 40 x 24), layouts
' scaled to it; a step is 1 or 2 pixels across in turn (1.5 on average:
' the C64 stepped 2 of its narrower pixels) and 1 down; the face under an
' object is read under its centre (the C64 read the character at the
' sprite's corner after a sprite-background collision); contact is a
' 13 x 13 box; the teleporter blinks every 12 ticks (the C64: every 9, too
' fast for the 3 Hz rule); the pause after a burst is 240 ticks in all
' (the C64's 464, almost 11 s); the arena is chosen on the title with the
' stick (the C64 used F1-F8 in its BASIC builder) and is built when the
' battle starts; at the end the winner is named, then the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 drives the blue craft, controller 2 the green
' craft (in ONE PLAYER mode either controller drives blue). The stick
' pushes the craft; a button with a direction throws the spheroid you
' carry. On the title, up and down choose a line, left and right change
' it, a button starts (or shows the credits).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

